  "rating": {
    "inherent": "critical",
    "residual": "critical",
    "forecast": "critical",
    "rationale": "Present risk is critical because an untrusted third-party MCP tool definition can directly steer a confidential local file read into irreversible external egress, and a second present path allows untrusted third-party output to be posted through shared Slack credentials into externally shared channels. Declared controls do not materially reduce these paths: the only approval is first-call remembered approval, which is not wired as a continuing approval gate and earns no residual credit. Forecast remains critical because the design already sits at the maximum tier; the single control whose decay or absence moves forecast most is the missing definition pinning / current use of @latest for quicknotes_mcp, since package or tool-definition drift can preserve and refresh the poisoning path without review. Manual validation applied from references/validation_checklist.md; confidence is limited to declared design artifacts, with no runtime observation."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "This assessment reflects declared design and configuration artifacts, not observed runtime behavior or live tool execution.",
    "No system prompt for the desktop client was provided, so instruction-level weaknesses beyond the quoted third-party tool description may be missing.",
    "No source code or runtime telemetry was provided, so approval UX details, exact argument visibility, and logging behavior are inferred from the runtime notes and kept at declared-design confidence.",
    "No explicit audit/logging artifact was provided; the repudiation property finding is based on absence in supplied artifacts rather than observed lack at runtime."
  ]
}
